---
layout: base.njk
title: "Insights"
summary: "Articles and updates from Natural Trace on molecular traceability, supply chain authenticity, and the natural products industry."
pagination:
  data: collections.insightsRegular
  size: 9
  alias: posts
permalink: "{% if pagination.pageNumber == 0 %}/insights/{% else %}/insights/page/{{ pagination.pageNumber + 1 }}/{% endif %}"
---
{#-
  Nine per page.

  Not for the reason that used to be written here. This said "nine because the
  grid is three across on a desktop, so a page is three full rows with nothing
  left hanging", and that was never true rather than having drifted:
  .insights-grid has been flex-direction:column inside an 800px cap since it
  was written on 9 July 2026, and this pagination was added on 12 August, a
  month later. A page has always been nine cards in one column. There were
  never any rows, so there was never a number of them to fill.

  What nine actually decides is how long one page is and how many pages the
  archive splits into, and nothing in the layout argues for that number over
  eight or twelve. It is a scroll-length judgement and it is open to being
  changed on that basis. What it is not is a constraint coming from the grid.

  Only the chronological articles are paginated. The pinned one, "Welcome to
  Natural Trace Insights", is evergreen rather than news and is appended to the
  last page, where an introduction to an archive belongs.

  The second half of that argument has expired and is recorded here so it is
  not reinstated. It ran that holding the pinned article out of the pagination
  also made the pages come out even, because eighteen dated articles were
  exactly two pages where nineteen gave a third page holding one item. The
  nineteenth dated article was published on 1 September 2026, so there are
  three pages now whichever way the pinned one is counted. The evenness is
  gone and is not recoverable by moving it; only the editorial reason above
  still holds.

  Page one keeps /insights/, so nothing linking to it has to change, and the
  rest are /insights/page/2/ onwards.
-#}

<section class="insights-listing">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="insights-header">
      <span class="section-label">Insights</span>
      {#- The heading says which page you are on, because "News & Updates" at
          the top of page three is disorienting and because two pages sharing a
          title is two pages competing for the same search result. -#}
      <h1>News &amp; Updates{% if pagination.pageNumber > 0 %} <span class="insights-page-of">page {{ pagination.pageNumber + 1 }}</span>{% endif %}</h1>
      {% if pagination.pageNumber == 0 %}
      <p class="insights-subtitle">The latest from Natural Trace: product developments, industry perspectives and company news.</p>
      {% endif %}
    </div>

    {% if posts.length > 0 %}
    <div class="insights-grid">
      {#- The newest article on page one is the lead, and reads sideways on a
          desktop: picture on the left, headline and summary on the right.

          Measured before it was changed, at 1440x860. The first headline on the
          page sat at 665px, so 77% of what a visitor saw on opening Insights
          was a label, the words "News & Updates", one line of subtitle and a
          cropped photograph. Nothing on the first screen said what the news
          was. On a shorter laptop the headline fell off the bottom entirely,
          which is where this started.

          Not the top padding, which is the obvious suspect and is innocent. The
          128px above the label is clearance for an 81px fixed nav, so there are
          only 47px of real gap up there and taking it would crowd the nav for
          about two lines of gain.

          Page one only. Page two opens on 2025 and page three on 2022; giving
          the oldest thing in the archive the largest card on the page would be
          promoting it for no reason other than where the pager put it. -#}
      {% for post in posts %}
      <article class="insight-card{% if pagination.pageNumber == 0 and loop.first %} insight-card--lead{% endif %}">
        {% if post.data.image %}
        <div class="insight-card-image">
          <a href="{{ post.url }}">
            <img src="{{ post.data.image }}" alt="{{ post.data.title }}" loading="lazy">
          </a>
        </div>
        {% endif %}
        <div class="insight-card-content">
          <time datetime="{{ post.date | date('%Y-%m-%d') }}">{{ post.date | date('%B %d, %Y') }}</time>
          <h2><a href="{{ post.url }}">{{ post.data.title }}</a></h2>
          {% if post.data.summary %}
          <p>{{ post.data.summary }}</p>
          {% endif %}
          {% if post.data.tags %}
          <div class="insight-tags">
            {% for tag in post.data.tags %}
            <span class="insight-tag">{{ tag }}</span>
            {% endfor %}
          </div>
          {% endif %}
          <a href="{{ post.url }}" class="read-more">Read more</a>
        </div>
      </article>
      {% endfor %}

      {#- The evergreen introduction, on the last page only. Repeating it on
          every page would be noise, and putting it on the first would push
          this month's news below a welcome message written in July. -#}
      {% if not pagination.href.next %}
      {% for post in collections.insightsPinned %}
      <article class="insight-card insight-card--pinned">
        {% if post.data.image %}
        <div class="insight-card-image">
          <a href="{{ post.url }}"><img src="{{ post.data.image }}" alt="{{ post.data.title }}" loading="lazy"></a>
        </div>
        {% endif %}
        <div class="insight-card-content">
          <time datetime="{{ post.date | date('%Y-%m-%d') }}">{{ post.date | date('%B %d, %Y') }}</time>
          <h2><a href="{{ post.url }}">{{ post.data.title }}</a></h2>
          {% if post.data.summary %}<p>{{ post.data.summary }}</p>{% endif %}
          {% if post.data.tags %}
          <div class="insight-tags">
            {% for tag in post.data.tags %}<span class="insight-tag">{{ tag }}</span>{% endfor %}
          </div>
          {% endif %}
          <a href="{{ post.url }}" class="read-more">Read more</a>
        </div>
      </article>
      {% endfor %}
      {% endif %}
    </div>

    {% if pagination.pages.length > 1 %}
    {#- A numbered pager rather than "load more". Every page is a real URL that
        can be linked, bookmarked and indexed, and someone who wants 2022 can
        jump to the last page instead of pressing a button eight times. -#}
    <nav class="insights-pager" aria-label="Insights pages">
      {% if pagination.href.previous %}
      <a class="insights-pager-step" href="{{ pagination.href.previous }}" rel="prev">
        <span aria-hidden="true">&larr;</span> Newer
      </a>
      {% else %}
      <span class="insights-pager-step is-off"><span aria-hidden="true">&larr;</span> Newer</span>
      {% endif %}

      <ol class="insights-pager-numbers">
        {% for entry in pagination.pages %}
        <li>
          {% if loop.index0 == pagination.pageNumber %}
          <span class="insights-pager-num is-current" aria-current="page">{{ loop.index }}</span>
          {% else %}
          <a class="insights-pager-num" href="{{ pagination.hrefs[loop.index0] }}"
             aria-label="Page {{ loop.index }}">{{ loop.index }}</a>
          {% endif %}
        </li>
        {% endfor %}
      </ol>

      {% if pagination.href.next %}
      <a class="insights-pager-step" href="{{ pagination.href.next }}" rel="next">
        Older <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
      </a>
      {% else %}
      <span class="insights-pager-step is-off">Older <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></span>
      {% endif %}
    </nav>
    {% endif %}

    {% else %}
    <div class="insights-empty">
      <h2>Coming Soon</h2>
      <p>We're preparing our first insights. Check back soon for news, updates, and industry perspectives from the Natural Trace team.</p>
    </div>
    {% endif %}
  </div>
</section>

{#- Posts that were only ever going to be LinkedIn posts.
    Content comes from src/_data/linkedin.yml, edited under "From our LinkedIn"
    in the CMS. Nothing is fetched from LinkedIn at build time: an editor
    chooses a post and pastes its address. LinkedIn publishes no feed of a
    company page's posts, and the API that would answer for one needs an app
    review and a key that expires every sixty days, which is not a thing to put
    in a build that four people share.

    Below the articles and the pager, not above them. The articles are the
    version of record (docs/publishing-to-linkedin.md sets out why) and this is
    an aside to them; the same argument the pinned-post comment above makes
    about not pushing this month's news down the page applies here.

    First page only. Page four is the archive for 2022 and has no business
    ending with this month's LinkedIn posts.

    A button rather than the post itself, which is the part most likely to look
    like an oversight later. Loading the frame loads a LinkedIn page, and that
    sets LinkedIn's cookies for every visitor who reaches this page, whatever
    they answered in the consent bar. The bar covers our own analytics and says
    so; stretching that answer to cover somebody else's tracking would make the
    wording untrue. So the frame waits to be asked, and the asking is the
    consent. With JavaScript off, or if main.js fails to load, the link below
    the button still goes to the post. -#}
{% if pagination.pageNumber == 0 and linkedin.posts and linkedin.posts.length > 0 %}
<section class="linkedin-strip">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="insights-header">
      <span class="section-label">LinkedIn</span>
      <h2>{{ linkedin.heading }}</h2>
      {% if linkedin.subheading %}<p class="insights-subtitle">{{ linkedin.subheading }}</p>{% endif %}
    </div>

    <div class="linkedin-grid">
      {% for post in linkedin.posts %}
      <div class="linkedin-embed"
           data-embed-src="{{ post.url | linkedinEmbed }}"
           data-embed-title="LinkedIn post: {{ post.title }}">
        <p class="linkedin-embed-label">{{ post.title }}</p>
        <button type="button" class="linkedin-embed-load">Show this post</button>
        <p class="linkedin-embed-note">
          Loads the post from LinkedIn, which sets its own cookies.
          <a href="{{ post.url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open it on LinkedIn instead</a>
        </p>
      </div>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
{% endif %}
